On Jul 13, 12:26 am, seryb...@gmail.com wrote:
> Are there programs about new special levels in main dungeon?
> Something like orc caves or elven halls in crawl...

I'm working on some stuff like that. My current plan is to do an
extensive "Lost World" branch a la The Lost World, The World That Time
Forgot, The Mysterious Island, et cetera, along with dinosaurs,
apemen, giant man-eating plants, all that. I don't think that adding
additional special rooms or the like to the main dungeon is a great
idea. It is already pretty well special roomed out. Main dungeon
creeping featurism is not warranted. But I think that there is
tremendous opportunity in branches, particularly if they are just fun
places to go that are not necessary for ascension, or perhaps even a
distraction from it. The end of the Lost World branch might, for
example, be a bunch of blue clay littered with diamonds and so on,
which would be great for points, but not helpful in ascension. Depends
what you want out of the game.

A lot of people don't play the game to ascend. I did for a while about
half a decade ago, and ascended a handful of times, but I don't know
if I really have time for that now. I will play some but probably
spend more time hacking on the code. (I finished vanilla Angband a
couple of times, and *THAT* was time-consuming. Good God. Never
again.)

One thing that could be added to the scoring is whether you achieved
all of the game's subtasks. In the (incredibly ancient) "Colossal
Caves" (a.k.a. ADVENT or Adventure, the very first text-based
adventure game), the endgame scoring was based upon various things you
had to accomplish. To get the perfect 300 point total you had to
finish the game *and* finish all the tasks in the game, a few of which
were optional, and one of which (for 1 point) was obscure. NetHack
could keep track of whether you had achieved all the objective
subtasks - got the luckstone, did Sokoban, did Sokoban with no
negative luck, did the Castle, and so on - as well as your raw score,
whether you ascended, and the like.



